WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
And the Oscar goes to ...

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:02.940
Interstellar.

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.580
Ex Machina.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:04.380
The Jungle Book.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:06.080
Blade Runner 2049.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.040
First Man.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.200
Here are this years nominees for "Achievement
in Visual Effects"

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:20.120
The best way to start, I think, is just by
you telling me who you are, what you do ...

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:20.680
Okay.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.940
Well, my name is Niko Pueringer.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:25.180
I'm one of the founders of Corridor Digital,
which is a YouTube channel.

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:29.600
And we specialize in doing VFX action-heavy,
spectacle-heavy YouTube videos.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.140
Cool.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:36.960
So we're here today to talk about the movies that were nominated for an Oscar for Visual Effects.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:42.400
The whole thing with this movie is that it's
made to look like it was done in one take.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.520
It's not entirely revolutionary and unexplored
before, but they're doing more complicated

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.260
setups and scenes while still trying to maintain
that one take look.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.690
The challenge they have with that is that
these effects need to be invisible.

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.879
I would love to talk about this long run scene.

00:00:58.879 --> 00:01:02.340
If you look at the, the behind the scenes
shot and then the actual shot of the film,

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.080
you know, first you might notice like, oh,
there's some extra explosions added

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:06.439
or there's some extra soldiers.

00:01:06.439 --> 00:01:10.289
But what you might not notice is things like
the tire tracks from the car that they're

00:01:10.289 --> 00:01:13.500
driving have been removed or the dust being
kicked up from the tires.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.840
And that's much, much more complicated than
just adding another explosion in the background.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.780
The challenge with 1917 is doing these invisible effects, but doing them for long takes

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.979
and then having these hidden cuts be seamless.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.340
Because the moment you get one of these little
details wrong, you break the world.

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:34.280
So I think looking at the scene, what will
probably surprise most people looking at it,

00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:36.429
is that the majority of the scene, the vast
majority of it, is 100 percent CG.

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.009
The stormtroopers, the speeders, the sand,
all that kind of stuff.

00:01:39.009 --> 00:01:42.530
There's a phrase for things like
the dust clouds, and the physics and things

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:43.530
like that — the smoke.

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.380
It’s usually referred to as dynamics.

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.459
That's one of the first, very first giveaways
as a CG scene, is looking at the smoke and

00:01:49.459 --> 00:01:53.169
being kind of, like, painterly quality where
it’s not quite realistic – And we’re

00:01:53.169 --> 00:01:56.920
just kinda getting to the point where we can
kinda match real life with that.

00:01:58.760 --> 00:02:03.080
I think a lot of the hype that I heard was
definitely around these animals.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:06.940
But you were talking about how it's really
setting that impressed you.

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:11.120
I’d the biggest thing that stands out to
me — in any of these environment shots — is

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.549
the vast number of actual objects on screen.

00:02:14.549 --> 00:02:18.670
Like this, this shot of Mufasa and Simba walking
through the grass as we focus away from the

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:23.340
spider and onto them — there's thousands
of blades of grass in this scene.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.231
There's leaves — there's hundreds of leaves
on the bush from the camera, and so all of

00:02:27.231 --> 00:02:29.740
these things need to have an incredible amount
of detail.

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:32.100
That’s what stands out to me as being incredible
here.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.520
Yeah, the animation is great.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:34.520
The rendering?

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:35.240
Impeccable.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:36.400
The lighting simulation?

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:36.980
Great.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.620
Wonderful.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.100
You know, all that’s awesome.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:41.500
But the fact that all of it comes together
cohesively?

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:44.360
That’s way crazier to me.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:51.400
So there’s a couple things about Avengers
that – that are really ...

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:52.460
impressive.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.000
One of the first ones is the, the time travel
suits.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:57.880
All those suits that you see them wearing,
those are completely CG,

00:02:57.880 --> 00:02:59.500
and they look completely realistic.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:01.440
You cannot tell that they are fake.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.020
They replace the entire body except for the
head.

00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:04.140
And that's insane.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.140
Why would they do that?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:09.220
Well, it takes a lot of time to construct
these costumes for these characters, and it's

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.380
only being used for five minutes of the film.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:15.560
Is there anything like particular on the suits
that made them stand out to you?

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:21.300
So I was scrutinizing the suits when I watched
this scene again and the only things that

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.900
really give them as being CG — and you
have to really be looking for it and know

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.820
what you're looking for – is when they're
interacting with something real.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.720
So a suit really and only interacts with two
real things.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.840
The neckline of the actor and the feet on the
ground.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.140
You have to fake the shadow from the suit being cast
on the ground.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:40.300
And there's a lot of nuances to that.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.900
But ... Nobody’s analyzing the neck lines.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:43.060
Right.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.860
You know, to me, out of all the films I've
been nominated for an Oscar here for Best

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:54.260
Visual Effects, The Irishman has the most unique,
brand-new technology.

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.840
The reason they're going through all this
trouble ... is because they want to tell a

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.180
story of a character throughout his entire
life – and they need to get him at his,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.200
you know, when he was 60, when he was 50,
when he was 40, when he was 30.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.780
How do we let this guy shoot a movie like
this with actors not wearing any markers or

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.320
tracking suits?

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:13.960
And yet we somehow need to capture a full
3D-model of the face for every frame of the

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:17.900
movie – basically a full real time 3D motion
capture.

00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:22.180
They just lit the scene with infrared lights
from the "witness cameras," they’re called.

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.060
So they're seeing the scene with perfect lighting.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:28.480
And we can't see with our eyeballs, the actors
can't see the infrared light.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.240
The main camera that they're filming the scene
with can't see the infrared light, and it

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:32.580
was really, really smart.

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:37.320
They did encounter one problem, though, and
that is: old cars.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.480
Old car windshields and glass, they used to
use lead when they'd make the glass.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:43.540
Lead doesn't let infrared light through.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:47.600
So, they actually had remove all the windshields
from the cars at any time to see a car in

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:50.820
the film that windshield, the reflection of
the windshield,

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:52.120
that's fake.

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:54.380
Do people really care about this award?

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.920
You know, These are just five films, and a
good chunk of them are just kind of doing

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.520
the same techniques we've already done.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.480
In fact, when comes to visuals, there's
so much going on in the space – every year

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:09.240
you're building off of the knowledge and the
tools that you've gained from the previous year.

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:12.280
And so these, these more advanced techniques
that they're using for these movies, you know,

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.380
it's lending the visuals to looking a lot
more photo real ...

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:16.700
but we've seen it before.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:21.700
The Oscars, it's good recognition, but there
is so much more that drives an artist – at

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.100
least a visual effects artist – outside
of hoping to win an award like an Oscar.

